# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk
Cargo.lock.orig

# Spike crates. Each is its own cargo package, so its build output is not
# under the two anchored paths above. `/target/` and `/src-tauri/target/` are
# anchored deliberately - an unanchored `target/` would also ignore a source
# directory somebody legitimately named that - so each new package needs its
# own line, and `spikes/` is the one place new packages appear.
spikes/*/target/

# What a spike run on the box writes next to the package: its reports, and
# the captures they describe. The reports carry window titles and process
# lists, and a loopback WAV can carry other people's voices.
spikes/*/*.txt
spikes/*/*.wav
spikes/*/*.mp4
spikes/*/*.progress

# Tauri
/src-tauri/target/
/src-tauri/gen/

# Node / frontend
node_modules/
dist/
dist-ssr/
.vite/
coverage/
*.local
# `svelte-check --tsgo` writes a shadow TypeScript project here. The gate does
# not use that flag (see docs/ci-and-releases.md for why), so this directory
# should not appear; it is ignored anyway because when it does appear it holds
# stale copies of deleted components and fails the build over them.
.svelte-check/

# Logs
logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Recordings & local data — never commit video or user data
*.mp4
*.mkv
*.rofl
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db

# Fixture videos are the exception: small sample clips live in fixtures/
!fixtures/*.mp4

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
*client_secret*.json

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Claude Code session-local state (launch.json itself is a real project
# file and stays tracked)
.claude/*.lock
